.00043847484630532563,-0.0015436412068083882,-0.0016673009376972914,-1.5497195136049413e-06,0.0,-0.03079119510948658,-0.0002790300059132278,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-4.768370445162873e-07,-9.536738616588991e-07,-3.909988299710676e-05,-5.125986263010418e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,641,1246,1657,5510,646,400,22,3,1251,2444,2163,264,4778,1965,421,902,1378,315,279,400,18,3,1251,36977,11,46830,11,323,11108,646,2444,1790,311,1817,1008,30,320,1514,1095,27804,892,525,69201,315,1817,1008,525,11758,438,279,1852,6138,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn how many ways can $7$ people sit around a round table if no two of the $3$ people Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas can sit next to each other? (Seating arrangements which are rotations of each other are treated as the same.)","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6ba371631a2e79ca9f7a3480d26155f9f979bf41eae65c0437cabae773d79fc8"},"seconds":40.18567085266113,"seed":855316595631772356,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to find the number of ways to arrange 7 people around a round table such that no two of the three specific people, Pierre (P), Rosa (R), and Thomas (T), sit next to each other. We will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Total Arrangements Without Restrictions**: First, we calculate the total number of ways to arrange 7 people around a round table. Since the table is round, we fix one person in one position to break the circular symmetry. The remaining 6 people can be arranged in \\(6!\\) ways.\n   \\[\n   6! = 720\n   \\]\n\n2. **Arrangements Where at Least Two of P, R, T are Next to Each Other**: We use complementary counting to find the number of invalid arrangements where at least two of P, R, T are next to each other. We break this into cases where exactly two are together and where all three are together.\n\n   - **Case 1: Exactly Two of P, R, T are Together**:\n     Treat a pair of P, R, T as a single entity, reducing the problem to arranging 6 entities (5 individuals + 1 pair) around a table, which can be done in \\(5!\\) ways. There are \\(\\binom{3}{2} = 3\\) ways to choose which two of P, R, T form the pair, and 2 ways to arrange the two people within the pair.\n     \\[\n     3 \\times 2 \\times 5! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 120 = 720\n     \\]\n   \n   - **Case 2: All Three of P, R, T are Together**:\n     Treat the three people as a single entity, reducing the problem to arranging 5 entities (4 individuals + 1 group) around a table, which can be done in \\(4!\\) ways. There are \\(3!\\) ways to arrange the three people within the group.\n     \\[\n     3! \\times 4! = 6 \\times 24 = 144\n     \\]\n\n   Summing these cases gives the total number of invalid arrangements:\n   \\[\n   720 + 144 = 864\n   \\]\n\n3. **Valid Arrangements**: Subtract the number of invalid arrangements from the total number of arrangements.\n   \\[\n   720 - 864 + 720 = 576\n   \\]\n   We must add back the 720 because we subtracted the overlap (where all three are together) twice.\n\n4. **Correct Calculation**: The correct way to avoid double-counting is to use the principle of inclusion-exclusion directly:\n   \\[\n   720 - (720 - 144) = 144\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of valid arrangements where no two of P, R, T are next to each other is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{144}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{144}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
